the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $c(5d+1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{c}{c}\\cdot \\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5c}{c(5d+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-5c}{c(5d+1)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113315/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113315"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
